course: dictionary
lesson: 2802
title: Disrupt and its meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Disrupt is a verb. You can say disrupt, disrupts, disrupted or disrupting, depending on when it happens. To disrupt something is to stop it going on normally, usually by causing trouble, noise or problems. «When a fight disrupts a class, the whole group may lose time or treats.» The fight stops the lesson from going on as it should.
explain: Look at the words around disrupt to find its meaning. Trouble, noise and problems are the clues. «Snow can disrupt travel, so buses may not run.» Snow is the problem, and buses not running shows that normal travel has stopped.
explain: Disrupt has a second meaning, used in news and business. It can mean changing the way something works in a big, new way. «A new invention can disrupt an industry» means the new idea changes how that work is done. To choose the right meaning, swap in your own idea and check that the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Disrupt is a verb: disrupt, disrupts, disrupted, disrupting.
fact: «Disrupt» means to stop something going on normally by causing trouble, noise or problems.
fact: Phrases you may meet include «disrupt the class», «disrupt travel» and «disrupt sleep».
fact: «Disrupt» can also mean to change the way something works in a big, new way, such as a new invention disrupting an industry.
fact: Strikes, roadworks, storms and noise can all disrupt daily life.
exercise: «When a fight disrupts a class, the whole group may lose time or treats.» What does disrupts mean here? | *Stops the class going on normally | Starts a new lesson early | Helps everyone learn faster | Makes the class bigger
exercise: «Snow can disrupt travel, so buses may not run.» What does disrupt mean in this sentence? | Paint the buses | Stop the snow from falling | Make travel faster and cheaper | *Cause problems so travel does not run normally
exercise: «A new invention can disrupt an industry.» Which meaning of disrupt fits here? | To pause a game for a short break | To make a noise in a quiet room | *To change the way an industry works in a big, new way | To stop a machine from being made
test: «The storm disrupted the power.» What happened to the power? | The power got stronger and brighter | The power was painted green | *The power stopped working normally | Nobody noticed any change at all
test: Ruby says, «Noise can disrupt sleep.» What does disrupt mean in that sentence? | *Stop sleep from going on as it should | Make sleep longer and deeper | Give sleep a new name | Wake everyone up for breakfast
exam: «Roadworks and strikes can disrupt travel.» What does disrupt mean here? | Stop the rain from falling | Put the roadworks in order | *Cause trouble so travel does not run normally | Make travel cheaper
